2. PATHS AND DISTRIBUTIONS

Nobody knows where a stock will be in the future. To reason about it,
we describe the future in two ways.

A path is one possible route the price could take from today to some
future date. Each path is one possible future.

A distribution describes the spread of where the price could be on one
particular date, and how likely each level is.

These are different pieces of information. A distribution tells you
where the price might be on a given date. It says nothing about how it
got there. That difference drives the rest of these notes.

A model of the market is a rule for generating paths. Simulating
thousands of them to price a product is called Monte Carlo simulation.

2.1 OPTION PRICING BASICS

A call pays nothing if the price ends below the strike, and pays the
difference if it ends above. Since we don't know where the price will
end, the fair price of an option is its average payoff across every
possible ending price, weighted by how likely each one is.

So an option's price depends on the full distribution of where the
stock could end up, not on a single forecast.

Some options go further. Path-dependent options pay based on the route
the price takes, not just where it ends. For example, a barrier option
switches on or off if the price touches a certain level, and an Asian
option pays on the average price over time. To price these, you need
the odds of every possible path.


2.2 THE OPTION SURFACE

Exchanges publish option prices for many strikes and many expiry dates.
Together they form the option surface.

Comparing call prices at neighboring strikes reveals how likely the
stock is to end near each price on that expiry date. Repeat this across
all strikes and you recover the market's full distribution for that
date (the Breeden-Litzenberger idea). Do it for every expiry date and
you get one distribution per date.

What the surface does not tell us is how those dates connect: how the
price travels from one date to the next. Path-dependent options depend
on exactly that missing piece.


2.3 HOW BANKS PRICE EXOTIC OPTIONS

Banks choose a model to fill in the missing paths:

  1. Calibrate: tune the model until it matches the option surface.
  2. Simulate: generate thousands of price paths from it.
  3. Price and hedge: average each payoff over the paths.
  4. Reuse: send the same paths to risk, stress tests and AI hedging
     tools.

Everything after step 1 trusts the model. Any flaw in it spreads to
every price, hedge and risk number.


2.4 CURRENT MODELING APPROACHES

A good pricing simulator must pass two tests: no free money, and an
exact match to the option surface.

  - Classical and exact math methods (local volatility, martingale
    optimal transport) pass both tests. But they only work for one or
    two assets or dates, and local volatility is one fixed choice with
    no strong reason to prefer it.

  - AI generators scale to many assets and look realistic. But they
    were built for realism, not pricing. They either ignore the
    no-free-money rule or only discourage breaking it with a penalty,
    which leaves some free money behind.

No current method both passes the tests and scales.

2.5 WHY SMALL ERRORS MATTER

A small amount of leftover free money is not a small problem.

  - Free money can be scaled up: a riskless profit can just be repeated
    at larger size.
  - AI hedging tools trained on the simulator will find any leak and
    learn to exploit it. Those profits don't exist in the real market.
  - Nobody downstream knows how big the leak is, so a validation team
    has nothing to check.

When the rule at stake is the fundamental theorem of asset pricing,
"mostly satisfied" isn't acceptable.
